  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Prep ingredients: Crush or finely chop 2 garlic cloves. Cut the celery into 1cm chunks. Cut the capsicum into 1cm chunks. Peel the sweet potatoes, then cut into 1cm chunks. Trim the green beans, then cut into 2cm lengths. Drain and rinse the beans.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292020/photos/large/prep_ingredients-fa47e4c31db177ee94e2b4f1b583ed3b.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Start gumbo: Heat 1 tbs olive oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Cook the garlic, celery and capsicum, stirring occasionally, for 2 mins or until softened.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292021/photos/large/start_gumbo-6a3f1d280ed653259774237e38186503.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Add tomatoes: Add the sweet potato, half the barbecue seasoning and 3 tsp plain flour to the pan. Cook, stirring, for 2 mins or until fragrant. Add the beans, tomatoes, 1½ tbs tomato paste, 1½ tbs barbecue sauce and 500ml (2 cups) boiling water and bring to the boil.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292022/photos/large/add_tomatoes-234e56a474c6dd2a81b3bcf02b3a8318.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer gumbo: Reduce the heat to medium-high and cook, stirring occasionally, for 12 mins. Add the green beans and cook for a further 3 mins or until the beans are tender. Remove the pan from the heat. Taste, then season with salt and pepper.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292023/photos/large/simmer_gumbo-0c859406bfe88970b43008cf42fe8935.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Serve up: Divide the gumbo among bowls, scatter over ¼ tsp chilli flakes, if using, and enjoy!",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292024/photos/large/serve_up-06db54fcd86abd471732934c8539059f.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Did you know?: Gumbo is a speciality of Louisiana cooking, although its origins trace back to French, Spanish and African cultures. There are countless versions, some made with seafood or meat, while others include okra, which helps to thicken the stew. That's how the dish got its name – 'gumbo' is derived from the West African word for okra.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/292025/photos/large/did_you_know-b28d1116ff3d732d93870a7809c12902.jpeg"
    }
  ]
